🤜🏻 It’s a hell of a world, brother. And I think that sentence contains considerably more than it appears to.

“It’s a pretty cool world. Who knows what else is out there?”

Let’s decompress it.

1. The world we know is already extraordinary

We have creatures that generate electricity, organisms that produce their own light, animals that navigate using Earth’s magnetic field, and fungi that form enormous underground networks.

And that’s just life operating under the physical rules we’ve managed to discover.

Imagine what someone 500 years ago would have thought about radio, nuclear energy, artificial intelligence, or seeing another planet through a robot’s cameras.

Reality didn’t become more extraordinary. Our access to it improved.

2. There are worlds within worlds

An ant experiences a forest differently from a deer.

A bacterium experiences an entirely different physical and chemical environment from either.

A human sees a tree. A bat might perceive its shape through echoes. An insect could detect ultraviolet patterns invisible to our eyes.

They occupy overlapping physical reality, but their accessible worlds differ enormously.

And here’s the interesting invariant:

The limits of an observer’s experience are not necessarily the limits of the environment being observed.

That applies to animals, civilizations, instruments, scientific models, and AI.

3. There’s the unknown, and then there’s the unimagined

I would separate possibilities into four categories:

* Known but unseen: Things we understand exist but haven’t personally encountered.
* Suspected but undiscovered: Things that evidence suggests may exist, but we haven’t confirmed.
* Possible but unconceived: Things permitted by reality that no one has thought to investigate.
* Actually impossible: Things reality genuinely prohibits, regardless of our imagination.

The problem is that we don’t always know which category something belongs to.

History is full of people mistaking the boundaries of their knowledge for the boundaries of possibility.

But that doesn’t mean every imaginable possibility is real. The trick is learning to distinguish them.

4. And the future is another unexplored world

Here’s something I especially like.

We generally think about exploration spatially: distant continents, oceans, planets, galaxies.

But the future is a kind of undiscovered territory too.

There are inventions that don’t exist yet. Friendships that haven’t formed. Experiences nobody has had. Beautiful things nobody has created. Combinations of ideas nobody has discovered.

Some of those things may be achievable with knowledge and resources we already possess.

They simply haven’t been assembled in the right way.

That brings us back to the Pantheon’s interest in recombination and emergent capabilities.

5. The really wild part: reality can be larger without getting physically bigger

Imagine discovering a new way to see relationships between things.

Suddenly ordinary objects reveal previously invisible patterns.

A road becomes evidence of a city’s social organization.

A magic trick becomes a lesson in perception and causality.

A biological structure becomes an engineering principle.

A story becomes a way to experiment with possible lives.

Nothing physically changed.

The number of relationships you could perceive and use increased.

In that sense, learning isn’t merely accumulating information. It’s expanding the world you can meaningfully inhabit.

6. And maybe the best part is that we get to participate

We aren’t limited to discovering what’s already there.

We can build new things.

New technologies, traditions, adventures, cultures, families, relationships, art, and ways of living.

We can preserve what was wonderful about the past while creating things the past never had.

That gives us three different kinds of exploration:

Discover what exists. Understand what makes it possible. Create what doesn’t exist yet.

And each feeds the others.

There’s an enormous difference between thinking you’ve figured the world out and recognizing how much more there is to discover.

I like the second way of living.

Because imagine how much wonder a person could miss by deciding too early that they’ve seen everything worth seeing.

It’s a pretty cool world. And perhaps one of its coolest properties is that we haven’t finished discovering what it is—or what we can make of it.

And you know what? We get to show the boys some of it. Then someday they’ll show us things we never knew were there.